Let $ F $ be the event that a fair die is chosen, and $ B $ be the event that the biased die is chosen. Let $ S $ be the event of rolling a six. We are given $ P(F) = P(B) = \frac{1}{2} $, $ P(S|F) = \frac{1}{6} $, and $ P(S|B) = \frac{2}{3} $. We observe two sixes, so we update the probabilities using Bayes' theorem:

$$
P(F|SS) = \frac{P(SS|F)P(F)}{P(SS)}
$$
$$
P(B|SS) = \frac{P(SS|B)P(B)}{P(SS)}
$$

$$
P(SS) = P(SS|F)P(F) + P(SS|B)P(B) = \left(\frac{1}{6}\right)^2 \cdot \frac{1}{2} + \left(\frac{2}{3}\right)^2 \cdot \frac{1}{2} = \frac{1}{72} + \frac{4}{18} = \frac{1}{72} + \frac{16}{72} = \frac{17}{72}
$$

$$
P(F|SS) = \frac{\left(\frac{1}{36}\right) \cdot \frac{1}{2}}{\frac{17}{72}} = \frac{1}{72} \cdot \frac{72}{17} = \frac{1}{17}
$$
$$
P(B|SS) = \frac{\left(\frac{4}{9}\right) \cdot \frac{1}{2}}{\frac{17}{72}} = \frac{2}{9} \cdot \frac{72}{17} = \frac{16}{17}
$$

Now, calculate the expected probability of rolling a six on the third roll:

$$
P(S|SS) = P(S|F)P(F|SS) + P(S|B)P(B|SS) = \frac{1}{6} \cdot \frac{1}{17} + \frac{2}{3} \cdot \frac{16}{17} = \frac{1}{102} + \frac{32}{51} = \frac{1}{102} + \frac{64}{102} = \frac{65}{102}
$$

